#{[17592186064790 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x\\cos x}$?"] [17592186064791 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\cos x - 1 + \\sin^{2} x}{x}$?"] [17592186064792 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{t \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\sec t}{t\\sec t}$."] [17592186064793 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  1}(1 - \\cos (x - 1))/(1 - x)$."] [17592186064807 :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=3?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}, & x<3 \\\\ 0, & x=3 \\\\ 2x+3, & x>3\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186064807 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $f(3)=0,$ so $f(3)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(2x+3) \\\\  & =2(3)+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9,$ while $f(3)=0.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3).$"] [17592186064887 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos^{2} 2x}{3x}$?"] [17592186064888 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}({x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x).$"] [17592186065352 :tutorial/content "Consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1- \\cos x}{x}\n$$\n\nNotice that direct substitution of $x=0$ leads to the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} &= \\frac{1 - \\cos 0}{0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 1}{0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}\n$$\n\nwe get the following picture.\n\n![](images/8c/8c3e6395017a2d1206f10debb5e6feb0719556ed4e053c7dfe1fbb0c80bd61aa.png)\n\nWhile\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1 - \\cos{x}}{x}\n$$\n\nis undefined at $x=0$, it does appear from the graph that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}=0\n$$\n\nThis is indeed the case. Moreover, it's possible to prove this result rigorously. However, we will assume that it is true and use it to calculate other limits."] [17592186065353 :tutorial/content "We can prove the result\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}=0\n$$\n\nusing the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the numerator and the denominator of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}\n$$\n\nby $1+\\cos x$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} &= ((1 - \\cos x)(1 + \\cos x))/(x(1 + \\cos x)) \\\\\n&= (1 - \\cos^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x)) \\\\\n&= (\\sin^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now take the limit as $x\\to 0$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(\\sin^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(\\frac{\\sin x}{x} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{1 + \\cos x}) \\\\\n&= (\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\sin x}{x}) \\cdot (\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\sin x}{1 + \\cos x}) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin 0}{1 + \\cos 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{1 + 1} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186065373 :tutorial/content "We can use our special limit with cosine to evaluate other limits, such as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 3} \\dfrac{1-\\cos(x-3)}{2x-6}\n$$\n\nFor this limit, notice that as $x\\to3$, both the numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00,\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  3}(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(2x - 6) &= \\lim_{x \\to  3}(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(2(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  3}(\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(x → 3)(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/((x - 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, this limit looks very similar to our special limit for cosine. So, if we make the substitution\n\n$$\n\\theta = x-3\n$$\n\nthen, since $\\theta \\to 0$ as $x \\to 3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 3} \\dfrac{1-\\cos{\\color{blue}(x-3)}}{\\color{blue}(x-3)} = \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\: \\dfrac{1-\\cos {\\color{blue}\\theta}}{\\color{blue}\\theta} = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  3}(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(2x - 6) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(x → 3)(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/((x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186065378 :tutorial/content "Let's build some more intuition for why velocity is defined as the derivative of position.\n\nIf a car is moving at a constant velocity, and it travels a distance of $\\Delta x=100\\,\\textrm{m}$ in a time interval $\\Delta t=10\\,\\textrm{s},$ then we know that its velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{Δx}{Δt} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{10} \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut what if the velocity itself changes with time? Perhaps the car accelerates for a couple of seconds in the beginning, thereby increasing its velocity. In that case, the formula above only gives us the average velocity. \n\nHowever, if we take a smaller time interval, we can get more precise information about the velocity at a given time $t.$ To get the instantaneous velocity of the car, we need to take the limit as $\\Delta t\\to 0.$ When we do this, we get the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\lim_{Δt\\to 0}\\frac{Δx}{Δt} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous velocity is the number that you read on the speedometer of cars."] [17592186065391 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below.\n$$\nf(x)=\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}3 x + a , & x \\ne  4 , \\\\ 13 , & x = 4\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=4.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $a,$ we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $a$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x) = f(4)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $4.$ So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x),$ we need to use the function definition for $x \\neq 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 4}(3x+a) \\\\  & =3(4)+a \\\\  & =12+a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=4,$ the function is defined as $f(x)=13.$ So $f(4)=13,$ and we can solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ 12+a & =13 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=4,$ we need to choose $a=1.$"] [17592186065402 :tutorial/content "Remember that to test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1\\. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n2\\. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n3\\. Does $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?\n$$\n\nLet's use these three conditions to show that the following function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 + x , & x \\le  1 \\\\ 3 - x & x > 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe check all three conditions in turn.\n\n1\\. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n  $$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\n  So, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n2\\. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n  $$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3-x) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  As a result:\n\n  $$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\n  Therefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists: \n\n  $$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 \\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n3\\. We have that $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 = f(1).\n$$  $\\:\\: {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c55aa89d279d94b7561f6c24ebdb37bafcf46dbc20edf4f93d515aca7ae4cde6.png)"] [17592186065443 :tutorial/content "Adding decimals is no harder than adding whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's find $0.4 + 2.6$.\n\nFirst, we write one number above the other, lining up the decimal points and placing a decimal point below the others in our answer:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 4 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 6;\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers. After each addition, we write the ones place and carry the tens place of the result.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} &  &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.4 + 2.6 = 3\n$$"] [17592186065560 :answer/value "-5\\ln|x + 2 | + \\frac{2}{x + 2} - 2\\ln | x - 3|+ K"] [17592186065561 :answer/value "2\\ln|x + 2 | -\\frac{2}{x - 3} + 5\\ln | x - 3|+ K"] [17592186065562 :answer/value "-2\\ln|x - 3 | + \\frac{3}{x - 3} + 2\\ln | x + 2|+ K"] [17592186065563 :answer/value "-2\\ln|x + 2 | + \\frac{5}{x + 2} + 2\\ln | x - 3|+ K"] [17592186065564 :answer/value "5\\ln|x + 2 | + \\frac{2}{x - 3} + 2\\ln | x - 3|+ K"] [17592186066016 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x) = \\frac{π}{2}, \\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x) = π"] [17592186066017 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x) = π, \\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x) = \\frac{π}{2}"] [17592186066018 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x) =-\\frac{π}{2}"] [17592186066019 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x) = 0"] [17592186066020 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x) = \\frac{π}{2}"] [17592186066022 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1^{-}}f(x) = DNE, \\lim_{x \\to  2^{+}}f(x) = DNE"] [17592186066023 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1^{-}}f(x) = 1, \\lim_{x \\to  2^{+}}f(x) = 2"] [17592186066024 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1^{-}}f(x) = 2, \\lim_{x \\to  2^{+}}f(x) = 1"] [17592186066025 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1^{-}}f(x) = DNE, \\lim_{x \\to  2^{+}}f(x) = 2"] [17592186066026 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1^{-}}f(x) = 1, \\lim_{x \\to  2^{+}}f(x) = DNE"] [17592186066040 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x) = π, \\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x) = \\frac{π}{2}"] [17592186066041 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x) = \\frac{π}{2}, \\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x) = DNE"] [17592186066042 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x) = \\frac{π}{2}, \\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x) = π"] [17592186066043 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x) = 2π, \\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x) = π"] [17592186066044 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x) = π, \\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x) = DNE"] [17592186066046 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  (1/2)^{+}}f(x) = DNE, \\lim_{x \\to  1^{-}}f(x) = DNE"] [17592186066047 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  (1/2)^{+}}f(x) = 0, \\lim_{x \\to  1^{-}}f(x) = DNE"] [17592186066048 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  (1/2)^{+}}f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}, \\lim_{x \\to  1^{-}}f(x) = 1"] [17592186066049 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  (1/2)^{+}}f(x) = 0, \\lim_{x \\to  1^{-}}f(x) = 0"] [17592186066050 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  (1/2)^{+}}f(x) = DNE, \\lim_{x \\to  1^{-}}f(x) = 0"] [17592186067481 :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}|x+5|-\\frac{3}{x+5}+2\\operatorname{ln}|x+4|+C"] [17592186067482 :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}|x+5|+\\frac{2}{x+5}-\\operatorname{ln}|x+4|+C"] [17592186067483 :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}|x+5|+\\frac{3}{x+5}+\\operatorname{ln}|x+4|+C"] [17592186067484 :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}|x+5|+\\frac{2}{x+5}-3\\operatorname{ln}|x+4|+C"] [17592186067485 :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}|x+4|-\\frac{2}{x+5}-3\\operatorname{ln}|x+5|+C"] [17592186067547 :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}|x|-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}|x-1|+C"] [17592186067548 :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}|x|-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}|x-1|+C"] [17592186067549 :answer/value "\\ln|x | -5\\ln | x - 1|+ C"] [17592186067550 :answer/value "5\\ln|x | -\\ln | x - 1|+ C"] [17592186067551 :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}|x|-5\\operatorname{ln}|x-1|+C"] [17592186068428 :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)\n$$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/eead78a813f820cf12cbd05bd22b230b1753c7e4ebe1e77d2c1eb6b61c11f4ed.png)"] [17592186068428 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$"] [17592186068471 :question/worked-solution "To simplify the expression, we first factor the numerator and denominator under the radical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{x^{2} - 4x + 4}{x^{2} + 6x + 9}} &= \\sqrt{(x^{2} + 2 \\cdot x \\cdot (-2) + (-2)^{2})/(x^{2} + 2 \\cdot x \\cdot 3 + 3^{2})} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{((x - 2)^{2})/((x + 3)^{2})}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by applying the quotient rule for radicals and the rules of absolute value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{(x+3)^{2}}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}}}{\\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x-2|}{|x+3|} \\\\  & =|\\frac{x-2}{x+3}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186068485 :question/worked-solution "Using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4z^{2} - 25 &= 0 \\\\\n(2z)^{2} - (5)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(2z - 5)(2z + 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n2z-5=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 2z+5=0\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-5 & =0 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & 2z+5 & =0 \\\\ 2z & =5 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & 2z & =-5 \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2} & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & z & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nz = -\\dfrac 5 2, \\dfrac 5 2\n$$\n\nWe can write this even more concisely as\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\dfrac 5 2\n$$"] [17592186068533 :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0} f(x)\n$$ for the function below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0ed78e2e640611d70263e17a76eb4a13e9e2816efba02494b53e540d38065d6.png)"] [17592186068533 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $0,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)= 1\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(0)$ is $2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $1$."] [17592186068534 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below? \n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)\n$$\n\n![](images/68/68241f3cd913cd91d10765499dc4ce4398fb3e8a81ec4bcfb65b47c0d77731d6.png)"] [17592186068534 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $3,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSimiliarly, as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)\\ne\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."] [17592186068546 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $0$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{-}}f(x) = 1 \\, .\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{+}}f(x) = 1 \\, .\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{+}}f(x)=1 \\,.\n$$"] [17592186068549 :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$"] [17592186068549 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can see that the left and right-sided limits are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false.  The graph of the function breaks at $x=2.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."] [17592186068551 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3^+} f(x)=f(3)=1.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(f(x)-{x}^{2})=1-{3}^{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186068635 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below? \n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)\n$$\n\n![](images/1c/1c94c7d99009c46023556ce0f4f662f5a90ad315048099394016390552ade809.png)"] [17592186068635 :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the limits in question.\n\nLooking at the graph, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ However, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=0, \\qquad \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\text{DNE} \\, .\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined, and as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)=\\text{DNE}, \\qquad \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)=0 \\, .\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)\n$$ becomes $0=0,$ which is true.\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)\n$$ becomes $\\text{DNE}=0,$ which is false.\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)\n$$ becomes $\\text{DNE}=\\text{DNE},$ which is false because we can't compare the undefined values.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186068649 :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that, approaching $x=-2$ from the right, the function approaches the value $y=4$. However, we cannot approach $x=-2$ from the left, because the function is not defined left of $x=-2.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,-2^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,,\\quad\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,-2^{+}}f(x)=4 \\,.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand,  approaching $x=2$ from the left, the function approaches the value $y=2$. However, we cannot approach $x=2$ from the right, because the function is not defined right of $x=2.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2^{-}}f(x)=2\\,,\\quad\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\,2^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE} \\,.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Here, $\\text{DNE}$ is simply short for \"does not exist\"."] [17592186068659 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\cos(\\theta)$ ranges between $-1$ and $1$. So we have\n\n$$\n-1 \\le \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) \\le 1\n$$\n\nand multiplying both sides by $x^4$ gives\n\n$$\n-x^4 \\le x^4\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) \\le x^4\n$$\n\nNow, taking the limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{x \\to  0}(-x^{4}) \\le & \\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{4}\\cos \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\le lim_(x → 0)(x^{4}) \\\\ 0 \\le & \\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{4}\\cos \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich, by the squeeze theorem, implies that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to0} x^4\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = 0\n$$"] [17592186068661 :question/worked-solution "Taking the limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{x \\to  2}(4x - 4) \\le & \\lim_{x \\to  2}f(x) \\le lim_(x → 2)x^{2} \\\\ 4(2) - 4 \\le & \\lim_{x \\to  2}f(x) \\le 2^{2} \\\\ 4 \\le & \\lim_{x \\to  2}f(x) \\le 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the squeeze theorem, we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 2} f(x)=4\n$$"] [17592186068664 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\cos(\\theta)$ ranges between $-1$ and $1$. So we have\n\n$$\n-1 \\le \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) \\le 1\n$$\n\nNow, since $e^x$ is a positive, increasing function, we can exponentiate both sides to give\n\n$$\ne^{-1} \\le e^{\\cos (1/x)} \\le e^{1}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the inequality by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\nx^2e^{-1} \\le x^2e^{\\cos (1/x)} \\le x^2e^{1}\n$$\n\nand taking the limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{2}e^{-1} \\le & \\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{2}e^{\\cos (1/x)} \\le lim_(x → 0)x^{2}e^{1} \\\\ 0 \\le & \\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{2}e^{\\cos (1/x)} \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the squeeze theorem, we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0} x^2e^{\\cos (1/x)} = 0\n$$"] [17592186068665 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\sin(\\theta)$ ranges between $-1$ and $1$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)}\\right|\\leq 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $\\mid x^3 \\mid$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{x}^{3}|\\cdot |\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)|\\le |{x}^{3}| \\\\ |{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)|\\le |{x}^{3}|,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand using the definition of absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n-|x^3| \\leq x^3 \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)} \\leq |x^3|.\n$$\n\nNote that we're using absolute value here because $x^3$ has an odd power.\n\nNow, taking the limit as $x\\to 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{x \\to  0}(-\\mid x^{3} \\mid) \\le & \\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{3}\\sin \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right) \\le lim_(x → 0)(\\mid x^{3} \\mid) \\\\ 0 \\le & \\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{3}\\sin \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right) \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the squeeze theorem, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0} x^3 \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)} = 0\n$$"] [17592186069091 :question/problem "![](images/15/159749b3bcae3d28f469dfd7356e4955721d23a40a45142a65aa6f7beccb90a7.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  0}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above."] [17592186069092 :question/problem "![](images/39/392fb6eaebb1e2faee96796f5633cfcd4e5d4c55484f9d185d5f55eb87d95fb5.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  1}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above."] [17592186069093 :question/problem "![](images/38/384a9198f7b164fce69e5c33a70ed1a4b6bf8d3c0d5c5a58fb8c9202b87f3d61.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  3}f(x)$ for the function above."] [17592186069094 :question/problem "![](images/09/09b0beda6782f188a57716ae41971a1167d3c931b9739f7e8ee6df59d87b82b2.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  2}f(x)$ for the function above."] [17592186069097 :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce76137f4cde1447b2dff893897ff101c9075e49cae5b2ddedb7c83a5e552028.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given above."] [17592186069101 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c9b656893111fd16146ab5b44465c7823d9cf6d82a30d2a3ca04cb0b389cf07.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x \\to  \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given above."] [17592186069218 :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b3544694cc41819941bdc05e2e1c9f388c5c538dbc5a58dbf02f6ae0bc685f7.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x)$, then$\\lim_{x \\to  1^{-}}f(x) =$\n{{field-1}} ,\n$\\lim_{x \\to  1^{+}}f(x) =$\n{{field-2}}."] [17592186069232 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[({x}^{2}-1)^{2}-1]}^{′}}{{[{x}^{2}]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4x({x}^{2}-1)}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}-4x}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}-4x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[4{x}^{3}-4x]^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{12{x}^{2}-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(0)^{2}-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069233 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\frac{x + 1}{\\sqrt{x} - 1}$?"] [17592186069243 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\sin x - x}{x^{3} + x^{2}}$?"] [17592186069247 :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\left(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{\\left(\\frac{1}{0+1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069248 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069274 :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f37f1f1e1b575695c38665ba5cc7cac694f441a2acb64d508f274825d2a4e12.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}f(x) = 2$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  0}f(x) = 2$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  1}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to  3}f(x)$"] [17592186069276 :question/problem "![](images/22/225b05930bec6085ed259aeffa9b729bb233782fb15c846eb78ab6b6fb8bcbbc.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  - 2}f(x) = 0$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  - 3}f(x) =-2$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  - 2}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to  2}f(x)$"] [17592186069304 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{x^{2} - x}{\\tan x}$?"] [17592186069311 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=f(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(f(x)+f(2)) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)+f(2) \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069315 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x = 4$ and $f(4) = 1$, then what is $\\lim_{x \\to  4^{+}}(f(x) + \\sqrt{x})/(3x)$?"] [17592186069320 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{2}e^{\\sin (1/x)}$."] [17592186069321 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  4}f(x)$ if\n$4x - 9 \\le f(x) \\le x^{2} - 4x + 7$."] [17592186069322 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  2}f(x)$ if\n$\\ln (x - 1) + 1 \\le f(x) \\le x - 1$."] [17592186069359 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}x\\sin \\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)$."] [17592186069360 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{2}\\cos \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)$."] [17592186069361 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{2}\\sin \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)$."] [17592186069362 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{4}e^{3\\cos (1/x)}$."] [17592186069363 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}x^{5}\\sin \\left(\\frac{4}{x}\\right)$."] [17592186069364 :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e689136f958f2d6425d36fa5448268bd645ffc7729a992d8c6621e2c51e3bfb.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  1^{+}}f(x) = 1$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  2^{+}}f(x) = 1$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  (-1)^{-}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to  (-1)^{+}}f(x)$"] [17592186069435 :question/problem "![](images/a0/a090fc9f29c025635de280fee0ba0194236ea8ebb3945bf2306e65c104949d32.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  - 1^{+}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to  0^{-}}f(x)$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  - 1^{+}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to  0^{+}}f(x)$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  2^{+}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to  2^{-}}f(x)$"] [17592186069473 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0^{+}}(\\ln x)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right))$."] [17592186069507 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{csc}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069924 :tutorial/content "The limit of a function at some point has **nothing to do** with the value of the function at that point.\n\nFor example, the following functions $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ have *different values* at $x=2,$ but they have the *same limit* at $x=2.$\n\n![](images/6a/6a73b74072a8a990c91a38553d519f4913370d34ef0251b7e45cc664da40517d.png)\n\n![](images/06/06e150aacae774b4fe883a2750ef13cebbddf2ea871e5a682f2587c47a54615a.png)\n\nThe actual values of $g(2)$ and $h(2)$ are irrelevant for determining the limit at $x=2$. All that matters is what values the functions *look like* they are approaching as $x$ approaches $2$. \n\nNear $x=2,$ both $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ appear to be approaching $y=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2}g(x)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2}h(x)=1 \\, .\n$$"] [17592186069928 :tutorial/content "The **limit** of a function is the value that the function approaches. For example, the function $y=f(x)$ shown below is not defined at $x=2,$ as represented by the open circle there $-$ but when we *approach* $x=2$, we see that $f(x)$ *approaches* the value $y=1.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7081d649c6cdfa0a19a0fb2de93b97ee700ad922f34530c7f3e5c2a3228c9fb.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$, the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ In other words, the limit of $f(x),$ as $x$ approaches $2,$ is $1$. We can express this mathematically as \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)=1 \\, .\n$$"]}
